## ABSTRACT
Hollow metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted increasing attention in the field of catalysis in recent years due to their unique cavity structure with fast mass-diffusion rates and easily accessible active sites. Here, we report the use of dynamic modulators, which are formed by the in-situ imine condensation reaction of 4-aminobenzoic acid and 4-formylbenzoic acid, to regulate the growth of MOFs to synthesize well-defined hollow thioether functionalized UiO-67 (denoted as H-UiO-67-S) single crystals. After supporting Pd nanoparticles, the designed catalysts Pd@H-UiO-67-S show excellent conversion (> 99.9%), selectivity (> 99.9%), and stability (10 cycles) in the selective hydrogenation of nitrobenzenes with other reducible groups. Density functional theory calculations and the experimental results reveal that Pd nanoparticles not only selectively adsorb the nitro-groups on nitrobenzene, but also restrict the adsorption of the aniline product, due to the interaction of thioether with Pd in the confined pores of H-UiO-67-S, finally result in a significant increase in selectivity of nitro-hydrogenation.
## KEYWORDS
hollow structure, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), dynamically modulated synthesis, selective hydrogenation, nitrobenzene
## 1 Introduction
As a key intermediate, substituted aniline prepared by selective hydrogenation of substituted nitrobenzene has attracted much attention in the fields of medicine, functional polymerization, dyes, and agricultural chemicals [1–3]. Recently, many progresses have been made in the selective hydrogenation of substituted nitrobenzenes using catalysts such as Au-based, Ag-based, or N-doped carbon nanotubes centered on Co [4–7], but the catalytic conditions required are usually harsh and require high temperatures (> 120 °C), high pressure (> 5 MPa), or prolonged reaction time (> 12 h). Although traditional noble metal catalysts such as Pd [8–11] and Pt [12,13] have high catalytic hydrogenation activity, it is difficult to achieve selective reduction of nitro-groups when substituted nitrobenzenes contain other reducible functional groups. The chemoselectivity of nitro-group reduction can be improved by modifying the noble metal catalyst with organic compounds [14,15], but the leaching of organic ligands during catalytic process leads to the easy decrease of selectivity [14]. Therefore, it is urgent to develop new methods for the construction of high-performance catalysts for selective hydrogenation of substituted nitrobenzenes under mild conditions while maintaining high activity and selectivity.Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which are formed by linking metal nods with organic ligands, have made great progress in the field of selective catalysis in recent years by engineering their catalytic microenvironments, such as metal node alternations, ligand functionalizations, and pore decorations [16–23]. Particularly, the hollow MOFs with unique cavity structures have attracted increasing research attention, owing to their inherent structural advantages, such as fast mass-diffusion rates, easily accessible active sites, and enrichment effect on reactants [24–26]. Generally, the most widely used preparation methods of hollow MOFs include interfacial synthesis method and template 