Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which are formed by linking metal nods with organic ligands, have made great progress
in the field of selective catalysis in recent years by engineering their catalytic microenvironments, such as metal node alternations, ligand  functionalizations,  and  pore  decorations  [16–23]. Particularly, the hollow MOFs with unique cavity structures have attracted increasing research attention, owing to their inherent structural advantages, such as fast mass-diffusion rates, easily accessible active sites, and enrichment effect on reactants [24–26]. Generally, the most widely used preparation methods of hollow MOFs include interfacial synthesis method and template synthesis method [27]. The interfacial synthesis method uses two insoluble phases to control the contact growth of metal ions and organic ligands at the interface to assist the formation of hollow MOFs [28,29]. While the template synthesis method requires the growth of MOFs on removable templates, and hollow MOFs with desired size and morphologies could be obtained after removing templates [30–32]. In addition, partial etching method [33,34], competitive coordination methods [35–37], and phase transformation method [38,39] have also been developed to construct hollow MOFs. Although lots of progress have been made, the as-synthesized hollow MOFs are usually in the forms of polycrystalline stacks with grain boundaries and defects [28]. Therefore, it is still a challenge to direct construction of hollow MOF single-crystals with well-defined morphologies and active centers [28].
To control the growth of MOF single crystal, modulated synthesis method is wildly adopted, especially in the case of Zr
based MOFs [40–43]. The modulators usually contain the same kind of coordination groups as the organic ligands, which competitively coordinate with the metal nodes to slow the growth rates of MOFs to form single crystals [35,44]. As a typical example, benzoic acid, acetic acid, and formic acid have been successfully used for the synthesis of \( \mathrm{Zr} \)-MOF single crystals with hundred microns scale, such as UiO-66, UiO-67, and UiO-68 [45, 46]. In addition, when the regulator occupies a specific MOFs plane, the crystal will grow in an anisotropic manner, which can control the crystal morphology [40, 47, 48].
## 2 Results and discussion
H-UiO-67-S and Pd@H-UiO-67-S were prepared using dynamically modulated synthesis method (Scheme 1). Typically, \( \mathrm{ZrOCl}_{2} \cdot 8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \), 4-aminobenzoic acid, and 4-formylbenzoic acid dissolved in N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) were first heated at \( 100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 5\,\mathrm{h} \) to assist the formation of the dynamic modulator cbaBA, then BPDC-S was added, followed by heating the solution at \( 80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 20\,\mathrm{h} \). To understand the formation mechanism of H-UiO-67-S, the reaction products at reaction time of 6 and \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \) were separated and studied by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), scanning electron microscopy (SEM), and transmission electron microscopy (TEM). At the reaction time of \( 6\,\mathrm{h} \), light yellow solids were obtained. SEM image of the sample found that they are spherical morphology, but the corresponding PXRD characterization shows the weak peaks, indicating the low crystallinity of the materials (Figs. S5(a) and S5(b) in the ESM). Interestingly, when the reaction time was extended to \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \), the intermediate products with partial hollow structures were clearly observed (Fig. S5(c) in the ESM). At the same time, the corresponding characteristic PXRD pattern of UiO-67 appears, indicating the successful formation of UiO-67-S (Fig. S5(d) in the ESM). Finally, well-defined high crystallinity H-UiO-67-S was obtained when the reaction time extended to \( 25\,\mathrm{h} \) (Fig. S6 in the ESM). The H-UiO-67-S presents a typical octahedral shape and the hollow character of H-UiO-67-S was confirmed from the SEM image with broken hole in the octahedra (Fig. S6(a) in the ESM), as well as the TEM images with uniform empty void (Figs. S6(b) and S6(c) in the ESM). The statistical average size of H-UiO-67-S is \( 304\pm29\,\mathrm{nm} \), and the average size of internal cavity is \( 195\pm27\,\mathrm{nm} \) (Figs. S6(d) and S6(e) in the ESM). In addition, the PXRD peaks of the dynamic modulator disappeared (Fig. S6(f) in the ESM), indicating the full replacement of cbaBA modulator with BPDC-S.
In addition, the dynamically modulated synthesis method has also been successfully used for the synthesis of other hollow MOFs, such as hollow UiO-68-NH\(_2\) and UiO-67-S(Hf). The hollow structures of UiO-68-NH\(_2\) and H-UiO-67-S(Hf) were proved by SEM and TEM images. The experimental PXRD patterns match well with their simulated results, indicating the high crystallinity of these hollow MOFs (Fig. S7 in the ESM).
Owing to the strong interaction of the other with metals, the as-synthesized H-UiO-67-S was used as an ideal platform for the
impregnation adsorption of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PdCl}_{4}\), after reduction by sodium borohydride, H-UiO-67-S with confined Pd NPs was prepared (denoted as Pd@H-UiO-67-S). The morphology and structure of the Pd@H-UiO-67-S were studied by SEM and TEM. As shown in Fig. 1(a), Pd@H-UiO-67-S still maintains the octahedral morphology after Pd immobilization, and the skeleton did not collapse, reflecting the stability of the hollow structure. The void and Pd NPs in the Pd@H-UiO-67-S could be clearly observed from a single hollow octahedron (Fig. 1(b)). The magnified TEM image shows the lattice fringes with interplanar distance of \(1.6\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 1(c)), which can be ascribed to the (111) planes of UiO-67, indicating the high crystallinity of the H-UiO-67-S. In addition, the Pd NPs are uniformly dispersed on H-UiO-67-S, and the embedded Pd NPs exhibit high crystallinity with interplanar spacing of \(0.23\;\mathrm{nm}\) [49], corresponding to the (111) plane of face-centered cubic palladium (Fig. 1(d)). According to statistical analysis, the average particle size of Pd NPs in H-UiO-67-S is \(2.1\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. S8 in the ESM). The high-angle annular dark-field scanning TEM (HAADF-STEM) image and corresponding energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) elemental mapping images of Pd@H-UiO-67-S confirm the uniform distribution of Zr, O, S, and Pd elements in the shell of the hollow octahedron (Figs. 1(e) and 1(f)).
The crystal structure of the obtained Pd@H-UiO-67-S was further characterized by PXRD, and the corresponding experimental diffraction peaks match well with simulated pattern, indicating the high crystallinity of the hollow MOF. And the diffraction peaks at \(5.6^{\circ}\), \(6.5^{\circ}\), \(9.2^{\circ}\), \(10.8^{\circ}\), \(11.3^{\circ}\), and \(13.1^{\circ}\) can be ascribed to the (111), (002), (002), (113), (222), and (004) planes of UiO-67 MOF, respectively. In addition, no obvious changes in the diffraction peaks were observed for H-UiO-67-S after the introduction of Pd NPs, indicating the stability of the hollow structure and the Pd NPs have no effect on their crystallinity (Fig. 2(a)).
Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was used to characterize the thermal stability of Pd@H-UiO-67-S. It can be observed from Fig. 2(b) that Pd@H-UiO-67-S and H-UiO-67-S both exhibit excellent thermal stability. The continuous heat loss before \(300\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) can be attributed to the loss of guest molecules. The skeleton of H-UiO-67-S begins to collapse under hot air at \(\sim400\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and collapses completely at \(\sim530\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). After loading Pd NPs, the thermal stability
of H-UiO-67-S is slightly decreased. In addition, the decomposition process of the hollow MOFs is very similar as that of Pd@UiO-67-S and UiO-67-S (Fig. S9 in the ESM), indicating that the hollow structure does not affect the thermal stability. Note that the remaining mass of Pd@H-UiO-67-S after the complete collapse of the framework is higher than that of H-UiO-67-S, due to the presence of Pd NPs.
The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) specific surface area and pore size of Pd@H-UiO-67-S were measured by \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption/desorption at \(77\;\mathrm{K}\). The adsorption–desorption curve of H-UiO-67-S is a typical type I isothermal curve of micropores materials and the calculated BET specific surface area is \(1395.22\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\). After loading Pd NPs, the BET specific surface area of Pd@H-UiO-67-S decreased to \(845.19\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\), owing to the occupying of MOF pores by Pd NPs. As a comparison, the BET specific surface areas of UiO-67-S and Pd@UiO-67-S are 1414.39 and \(864.44\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) (Fig. S10(a) in the ESM), respectively. However, hysteresis loops were observed evidently for H-UiO-67-S and Pd@H-UiO-67-S (Fig. 2(c)), which prove the existence of mesopores in the hollow MOFs. Accordingly, the pore size distribution of Pd@H-UiO-67-S and H-UiO-67-S shows that they not only have micropores with size of \(1.2\;\mathrm{nm}\), but also mesopores in the range of \(2{-}5\;\mathrm{nm}\), while Pd@UiO-67-S and UiO-67-S only have micropores of \(1.2\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. S10(b) in the ESM).
Finally, we studied the surface properties of the Pd@H-UiO-67-S catalyst by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The Pd 3d XPS spectrum of Pd@UiO-67 without thioether modification consists of asymmetric Pd \(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}\) and \(3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}\) peaks centered at 335.6 and \(341.0\;\mathrm{eV}\), respectively, which are the characteristic peaks of Pd\(^{\mathrm{[50-52]}}\). For Pd@H-UiO-67-S, the corresponding Pd 3d peaks shift to the 336.0 and \(341.5\;\mathrm{eV}\), respectively, because the electron-withdrawing effect of S reduces the electron cloud density of Pd (Fig. 2(e)). Correspondingly, the S 2p peaks of H-UiO-67-S centered at 163.2 and \(164.4\;\mathrm{eV}\) are shifted to 163.1 and \(164.2\;\mathrm{eV}\), respectively (Fig. 2(f)), owing to the increased electron cloud density of S [53, 54]. Therefore, thioether groups in the H-UiO-67-S can effectively regulate the surface electronic state of Pd NPs, which is important for catalytic applications.
It is generally believed that sulfur has a toxic effect on the catalyst, which leads to the deactivation of the catalyst. However, more and more studies have shown that the catalytic performance
of the noble metal catalyst with sulfur modifications could be significantly improved [55, 56]. For example, in the hydrogenation reaction of 1-phenyl-1-propene, the thiol-treated Pd nanosheets exhibited excellent catalytic selectivity \((>97\%)\) to the semihydrogenation product (1-phenyl-1-propene) [53]. Similarly, sulfur modification promotes the electrocatalytic alkyne semihydrogenation selectivity of Pd catalysts by weakening the alkenes adsorption and altering the adsorption model of alkynes [57, 58]. In another report, organic thiols modified Pt supported on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) could selectively hydrogenate the nitro-groups in 4-nitrostyrene in the liquid phase [14]. Therefore, we propose that Pd@UiO-67-S may be a high-performance catalyst for the selective hydrogenation of substituted nitrobenzenes.
To evaluate the catalytic performance of Pd@H-UiO-67-S, we chose 4-chloronitrobenzene as a model reactant, because both nitro- and chloro-groups can react with Pd NPs to form different products, while the selective hydrogenation of nitro-groups to synthesize substituted aniline under mild condition is of significant importance in fine chemical industry. In our experiment, 4-chloronitrobenzene hydrogenation was performed using Pd@H-UiO-67-S as catalyst at \(80~^{\circ}\!C\) and \(0.5\ \mathrm{MPa}\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) . As comparison, the performance of other catalysts, including Pd@UiO-67-S without the hollow structure and Pd@UiO-67 without thioether group, was also explored (Table 1, Entries 1–6, corresponding gas chromatograms are shown in Figs. S11–S16 in the ESM ). The characterization results of the Pd@UiO-67-S and Pd@UiO-67 catalysts are displayed in Figs. S17 and S18 in the ESM.
The average Pd sizes in Pd@UiO-67-S and Pd@UiO-67 are 2.4 and \(3.1\;\mathrm{nm}\) , respectively, which are close to that in Pd@H-UiO-67- S (Figs. S8 and S19 in the ESM). In addition, the contents of Pd in Pd@H-UiO-67-S, Pd@UiO-67-S, and Pd@UiO-67 measured by inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES) were \(1.87\;\;\mathrm{wt.}\%\) , \(1.77\;\;\mathrm{wt.}\%\) , and \(1.89\,\mathrm{\DeltaWt.\%}\) , respectively (Table S1 in the ESM). When used as catalyst, Pd@UiO-67 exhibits a high activity \((>\ 99.9\%\) conversion) for 4- chloronitrobenzene hydrogenation with the reaction time of \(^{1\mathrm{~h~}}\) , but the selectivity of 4-chloroaniline is only \(50.5\%\) , and dechlorination occurs to form aniline as a by-product (Table 1, Entry 1). In contrast, the thioether functionalized Pd@UiO-67-S showed greatly improved selectivity for 4-chloroaniline \((99.0\%)\) ,
but more reaction time \((2\,\mathrm{h})\) is required to realize full conversion (Table 1, Entry 3). Therefore, thioether functionalization of the UiO-67 significantly improves the selectivity, but it also has some negative effects on the reaction rates. Interestingly, the Pd@H-UiO-67-S catalyst exhibits an excellent conversion \((>99.9\%)\) and selectivity \((>99.9\%)\) at reaction time of \(1\,\mathrm{{h}}\) (Table 1, Entry 4). To identify the active sites of Pd@H-UiO-67-S, we performed temperature-programmed desorption (TPD) experiments for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) . As shown in Fig. S20 in the ESM, the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) -TPD of Pd@H-UiO-67-S has a broad desorption peak, while H-UiO-67-S does not detect any desorption signal, indicating that Pd nanoparticles are the active sites for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) dissociation. The peak at \(150\,^{\circ}\!C\) is attributed to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) desorption at metal Pd sites, while the other two peaks at 236 and \(250~^{\circ}\!C\) can be attributed to hydrogen spillage onto Zr-oxo cluster [59, 60]. It is reasonable that the unique hollow structure of Pd@H-UiO-67-S not only exposes more contact active sites, but also facilitates the diffusion of both reactant and product, which ultimately improves the reaction rates. More importantly, Pd@H-UiO-67-S can still maintain a selectivity of \(99.6\%\) for 4- chloroaniline even when the reaction time extends to \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) (Table 1, Entry 5). As a sharp contrast, the selectivity of 4-chloroaniline is decreased to \(2.3\%\) for Pd@UiO-67 after \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) (Table 1, Entry 2). To further explore the effect of thioether, benzyl methyl sulfide was added to the catalytic reaction system of Pd@UiO-67, and the selectivity of Pd@UiO-67 to 4-chloroaniline increased from \(50.5\%\) to \(99.1\%\) (Table 1, Entry 6). The result proved that the introduction of thioether does have an inhibiting effect on the dechlorination reaction of 4-chloroaniline. However, when the Pd@UiO-67 catalyst with additional benzyl methyl sulfide was reused in the repeated cycles, the selectivity of 4-chloroaniline dropped to \(83.0\%\) and \(68.3\%\) for the second and third cycle, respectively (Fig. S21(a) in the ESM). It may be due to the leaching of the thioether modifier attached to the surface of the Pd particles during the cycling process, which leads to a decrease in the selectivity for 4-chloroaniline [14]. In sharp contrast, Pd@H-UiO-67-S maintained about \(99\%\) of the conversion rate of 4- chloronitrobenzene and the selectivity of 4-chloroaniline after ten cycles (Fig. S21(b) in the ESM), and the slightly decreased conversion rate may be due to catalyst loss during recycle process. We also compared the catalytic performance of the Pd@UiO-67, Pd@UiO-67-S, and Pd@H-UiO-67-S catalysts at \(\sim\) \(27\%\)
adsorption energies of 4-chloronitrobenzene with different configurations are \(-1.43\), \(-0.44\), \(-1.09\), and \(-1.03\ \mathrm{eV}\), respectively (Fig. S25 in the ESM). Interestingly, the most stable adsorption configuration of 4-chloronitrobenzene is changed to vertically adsorb via \(-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) toward Pd (111) surface (Fig. 3(c)). This is mainly due to the steric hindrance effect caused by BPDC-S modification, which occupied the Pd surface in the confined pores of UiO-67, leaving insufficient available space for the horizontal lie down of 4-chloronitrobenzene. As a result, 4-chloronitrobenzene bonded on BPDC-S modified Pd (111) surface via the nitrogroup, and the planar characteristic as well as the C–Cl bond length were maintained (Fig. 4(c) and Fig. S23 in the ESM). More importantly, unlike the cases on bare Pd surfaces, the adsorption energy of the target product 4-chloroaniline on BPDC-S modified Pd surface dramatically decreased to \(-0.35\ \mathrm{eV}\) (Fig. 3(d)), which could hinder the subsequent dechlorination side reaction. Therefore, the BPDC-S modified Pd not only selectively adsorbs the nitro-group on 4-chloronitrobenzene, but also restricts the adsorption of the product 4-chloroaniline, which finally resulted in a significant increase in selectivity, matching very well with our experimental results (Table 1, Entries 3–6).
To exclude the influence of the benzene ring on the adsorption and desorption, nitromethane and aminomethane with simple structures were selected to further explore the adsorption capacity of the catalyst for nitro- and amino-groups. The adsorption energies of nitromethane and aminomethane are \(-0.72\) and \(-1.38\ \mathrm{eV}\), respectively (Figs. S26(a) and S26(b) in the ESM), confirming that the product aminomethane has a stronger binding energy with Pd after nitromethane reduction. For BPDC-S modified Pd, the adsorption energies of nitromethane and
aminomethane are \(-1.05\) and \(-0.67\ \mathrm{eV}\), respectively (Figs. S26(c) and S26(d) in the ESM), indicating that BPDC-S modified Pd had stronger adsorption with nitro-group and released amino-groups more easily. Briefly, these results are consistent with the case of 4-chloronitrobenzene and 4-chloroaniline (Fig. 3), i.e., the Pd without BPDC-S modification is easier to adsorb amino-groups on the product, while the modified Pd has stronger adsorption capacity to nitro-groups on the reactant and releases aminogroups more easily.
Given the high activity, selectivity, and stability of Pd@H-UiO-67-S for selective hydrogenation of 4-chloronitrobenzene, we further evaluate its application for selective hydrogenation of nitrobenzene containing other reactive functional groups. Firstly, the substituted nitrobenzene containing other halogen groups in different positions was explored, and it was found that 2-chloronitrobenzene, 3-chloronitrobenzene, and 2,4-dichloronitrobenzene can be completely converted into the corresponding halogen aniline with a selectivity of \(>99.9\%\) (Table 1, entries 7–9). When replacing \(-\mathrm{Cl}\) with \(-\mathrm{F}\) or \(-\mathrm{CF}_{3}\) groups, complete conversion is still possible with selectivity \(>99.9\%\) (Table 1, entries 10 and 11). In addition, substituted nitrobenzenes containing other functional groups including methoxy-, cyano-, hydroxyl-, methylamino-, formyl-, acetyl-, and benzene- could also be completely converted to the corresponding anilines with selectivity \(>99.9\%\) (Table 1, entries 12–18). The above results fully demonstrate the universality and durability of Pd@H-UiO-67-S on substituted nitrobenzene substrates, showing broad application prospects.
## 3 Conclusions
In conclusion, we synthesized hollow H-UiO-67-S single crystals by a facile dynamically modulated synthesis method. After loading Pd NPs, the Pd@H-UiO-67-S catalyst was prepared, which exhibited excellent conversion \((>99.9\%)\), selectivity \((>99.9\%)\), and stability (10 cycles) in the selective hydrogenation of substituted nitrobenzenes, and had a wide range of universality and tolerance to the reaction substrate. The interaction of thioether with Pd nanoparticles in the confined pores of H-UiO-67-S not only boosts the selective adsorption of the nitro-groups on nitrobenzene, but also restricts the adsorption of the aniline product, leading to the excellent performance of Pd@H-UiO-67-S. This work not only provides a simple and efficient method for synthesizing hollow MOFs single crystals, but also offers a new idea for improving the catalytic performance for selective hydrogenation reactions. We believe that our dynamically modulated synthesis method could be used for the synthesis of other hollow MOF single crystals toward many other selective reactions (e.g., selective hydrogenation of alkyne, diene, and unsaturated aldehydes/ketones).
